## In 30 seconds

FXML integrates via **DefaultFXMLModel** (FXML is the view), **embedded FXMLItem** in a Java View, **hybrid** key parts (`KEYPART_FXML_PREFIX`), or **fx:include** with sub-controllers. Controllers extend **AbstractFXMLController** to call `model()`.

Quick Start
=========================

FXML support is built into **core**. Add the **component** module if you use `StackModel` for FXML showcase-style navigation.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>core</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>component</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

**Minimal example — standalone FXML model (no Java View class):**

```java
// Model — FXML loaded by convention: Standalone.fxml in same package
public class StandaloneModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<StandaloneModel> {
    @Override
    protected void initModel() { }
}

// Controller — access the owning model via model()
public class StandaloneController extends AbstractFXMLController<StandaloneModel, View<StandaloneModel, ?, ?>> {
    @Override
    public void initialize(URL url, ResourceBundle rb) {
        model().titleProperty().set("Hello from FXML");
    }
}
```

Place `Standalone.fxml` next to the model on the classpath with `fx:controller` pointing to `StandaloneController`.


FXML Overview
=========================

JRebirth provides first-class support for integrating FXML files into its component architecture.
You can use FXML in several ways depending on how much control you need over the Java-side view logic:

* **Standalone (FXMLModel)** — Model loads FXML directly; no Java View class needed.
* **Embedded** — Full MVC pattern (Model + View + Controller) where the View loads FXML and embeds it in its node tree.
* **Hybrid** — `DefaultFXMLModel` with custom key parts pointing to a specific FXML path.
* **Included** — FXML files using `<fx:include>` to compose sub-views, with sub-controllers accessing the parent model.

All approaches leverage JRebirth's `AbstractFXMLController` which gives FXML controllers transparent access to the owning Model.


FXMLModel (Standalone)
=========================

The simplest way to use FXML with JRebirth. Your Model extends `DefaultFXMLModel` and JRebirth automatically loads the FXML file matching the model's package and class name (without the "Model" suffix).

No separate Java View class is required — the FXML **is** the view. The FXML controller should extend `AbstractFXMLController` to gain access to `model()`.


```java
// FXML path resolved by convention: StandaloneModel → Standalone.fxml (same package)
public class StandaloneModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<StandaloneModel> {
    @Override
    protected void initModel() { }
}
```

For `org.jrebirth.af.showcase.fxml.ui.standalone.StandaloneModel`, JRebirth loads `Standalone.fxml` from the same package on the classpath. Set `fx:controller` to your `AbstractFXMLController` subclass.

**Controller accessing the model:**

```java
public class StandaloneController extends AbstractFXMLController<StandaloneModel, View<StandaloneModel, ?, ?>> {

    @FXML private Label titleLabel;

    @Override
    public void initialize(final URL url, final ResourceBundle resource) {
        titleLabel.setText("Standalone FXML page");
    }
}
```


Embedded FXML
=========================

When you need a full MVC split (Model + View + Controller) but still want to use FXML for part of the view, you can **embed** FXML nodes into a Java-built View.

The View declares an `FXMLItem` resource and calls `.get()` or `.getNew()` to obtain FXML component instances which are then added to the node tree.

### Model


```java
public class EmbeddedModel extends DefaultModel<EmbeddedModel, EmbeddedView> {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        // Nothing to do yet
        super.initModel();

    }

}
```

### View


```java
public class EmbeddedView extends DefaultView<EmbeddedModel, BorderPane, EmbeddedController> {

    private static FXMLItem ROOT_EMBEDDED_FXML = Resources.create(new FXML("org.jrebirth.af.showcase.fxml.ui.embedded", "Embedded"));

    private VBox box;

    public EmbeddedView(final EmbeddedModel model) {
        super(model);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initView() {
        super.initView();

        this.box = new VBox();
        this.box.setAlignment(Pos.CENTER);

        final ScrollPane scrollPane = new ScrollPane();
        scrollPane.setPrefSize(600, 600);
        scrollPane.setContent(this.box);

        node().setCenter(scrollPane);

        addNode(EmbeddedView.ROOT_EMBEDDED_FXML.get().node());

        // Load 2 more instance
        addNode(EmbeddedView.ROOT_EMBEDDED_FXML.getNew().node());
        addNode(EmbeddedView.ROOT_EMBEDDED_FXML.getNew().node());

    }

    public void addNode(final Node node) {

        this.box.getChildren().add(node);

    }

}
```

Key points:

* `Resources.create(new FXML("package.path", "Name"))` declares an FXML resource item.
* `.get()` retrieves (or creates) the singleton instance.
* `.getNew()` always creates a fresh instance — useful for multiple copies of the same FXML component.


Hybrid FXML
=========================

The Hybrid approach uses `DefaultFXMLModel` but overrides the default FXML file path using key parts. This lets you reuse a single Model class to load different FXML files.


```java
public class HybridModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<HybridModel> {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        // Nothing to do yet
    }

}
```

The FXML path is passed as a key part when retrieving the model:

```java
Key.create(HybridModel.class, FXMLModel.KEYPART_FXML_PREFIX + "org.jrebirth.af.showcase.fxml.ui.hybrid.Hybrid")
```

The `KEYPART_FXML_PREFIX` constant signals to JRebirth that the key part value represents an FXML resource path rather than a normal component key.

**PageEnum wiring (FXML showcase):**

```java
public enum FXMLPage implements PageEnum {
    StandaloneFxml, IncludedFxml, ViewEmbeddedFxml, HybridFxml;

    @Override
    public UniqueKey<? extends Model> getModelKey() {
        switch (this) {
            case StandaloneFxml:
                return Key.create(StandaloneModel.class);
            case HybridFxml:
                return Key.create(HybridModel.class,
                    FXMLModel.KEYPART_FXML_PREFIX + "org.jrebirth.af.showcase.fxml.ui.hybrid.Hybrid");
            case IncludedFxml:
                return Key.create(IncludedModel.class, new LoremIpsum());
            default:
                return Key.create(EmbeddedModel.class);
        }
    }
}
```


Included FXML
=========================

When your FXML uses `<fx:include>` to compose sub-views, JRebirth automatically propagates the parent model to sub-controllers. Sub-controllers extending `AbstractFXMLController` can call `model()` to access the same Model instance as the parent.


```java
public class IncludedModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<IncludedModel> {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        // Nothing to do yet
    }

}
```

You can also pass a custom object (e.g. a data bean) as a key part to make it available in the model:

```java
Key.create(IncludedModel.class, new LoremIpsum())
```


FXMLController
=========================

JRebirth provides `AbstractFXMLController` as the base class for all FXML controllers. It implements `Initializable` and gives you:

* `model()` — access to the owning JRebirth Model.
* `view()` — access to the Model's View (when one exists).

Your FXML controllers should extend this class instead of implementing `Initializable` directly:

```java
public class MyController extends AbstractFXMLController<MyModel, MyView> {

    @Override
    public void initialize(URL url, ResourceBundle rb) {
        super.initialize(url, rb);
        // Custom initialization
    }
}
```

This bridges the gap between JavaFX's FXML controller mechanism and JRebirth's component architecture.


FXML Application Example
=========================

Here is how the FXML showcase application is bootstrapped, demonstrating the standard JRebirth application entry point:


```java
public final class FXMLApplication extends DefaultApplication<StackPane> {

    /**
     * Application launcher.
     *
     * @param args the command line arguments
     */
    public static void main(final String... args) {
        Application.launch(FXMLApplication.class, args);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public Class<? extends Model> firstModelClass() {
        return FXMLShowCaseModel.class;
    }

}
```

The `firstModelClass()` method returns the root model of the application, which in this case uses a `StackModel` to navigate between the different FXML integration examples (Standalone, Embedded, Hybrid, Included).


FXML Resources
=========================

JRebirth provides `FXMLItem` for typed, reusable FXML resource declarations. This approach is preferred over raw `FXMLLoader` usage because:

* Resources are managed by JRebirth's resource engine (caching, lifecycle).
* FXML paths are declared once and referenced by a typed constant.
* Module boundaries (JPMS) are respected via the resource API.

Declaration pattern:

```java
private static FXMLItem MY_FXML = Resources.create(new FXML("com.example.ui", "MyComponent"));
```

Usage:

```java
Node fxmlNode = MY_FXML.get().node();        // Singleton instance
Node freshNode = MY_FXML.getNew().node();    // New instance each time
```


